---
import LegalPage from '~/components/layout/LegalPage.astro';
import PlayCta from '~/components/article/PlayCta.astro';
import { defaultLocale } from '~/i18n/routing';
import { site } from '~/config/site';
---

<LegalPage
  locale={defaultLocale}
  title="About"
  description="What Rarest Answer is, how the rarest correct answer scores, and what this site does and does not claim."
  lastUpdated="2026-11-14"
>
  <p>
    {site.name} is the home of {site.game.name}, a free daily trivia game you play in the browser.
    A prompt asks you to name something that fits. Every accepted answer is correct. A rarer correct
    answer scores higher than the name almost everyone types first.
  </p>
  <h2>What you can do here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Play the shared daily round on the <a href="/">homepage</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep practicing in <a href="/unlimited/">unlimited mode</a>, or pick one category under trivia.
    </li>
    <li>
      Read the <a href="/guides/how-to-play-rarest-answer/">rules</a>, the
      <a href="/guides/how-to-find-rare-answers/">guide to finding rare answers</a>, and
      <a href="/guides/rarest-answer-strategy/">strategy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Compare other <a href="/guides/best-daily-trivia-games/">daily trivia games</a> and
      <a href="/games/">games like this one</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>How a correct answer scores</h2>
  <p>
    Scores use six tiers. Common is 5 points, Familiar is 10, Uncommon is 20, Rare is 40, Epic is
    70, and Legendary is 100. Tier 6 is the rarest. Once each round, a Spotlight boost doubles the
    points for a single prompt. The full table and the difference between the daily round and
    unlimited play are on the <a href="/guides/how-to-play-rarest-answer/">how to play</a> page.
  </p>
  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <p>
    {site.game.name} is an independent game. Pages about other games, including Krillion and the
    Roblox experience Rarest Answer Party, are unofficial guides. They are not written by those
    creators, and they are not affiliated with them, with Roblox, or with the shows and puzzles
    they mention. {site.legalNotice}
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not invent codes, point tables, release dates, or creator names. If a public page does
    not state a rule, the guide says so instead of filling the gap. The
    <a href="/guides/rarest-answer-party-roblox/">Roblox page</a> quotes the experience description
    and a dated read of Roblox's games API. Live player counts on that page will move.
  </p>
  <h2>Where the answers come from</h2>
  <p>
    Each prompt has a fixed list of accepted answers and a rarity rank inside that list. The
    published set is compiled from Wikidata (CC0) and monthly Wikipedia or Wiktionary pageviews.
    "Name a species of great ape" is one of those prompts: eight living species, with the Tapanuli
    orangutan in the rarest tier. The
    <a href="/guides/how-to-find-rare-answers/">finding guide</a> uses the names and the tier order,
    and it says when a biological fact comes from Wikipedia instead. The Stranger Things list is a
    scored prompt too, on the
    <a href="/answers/stranger-things-characters/">characters page</a>, ranked by pageviews rather
    than by billing.
  </p>
  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    You do not need an account to read the guides or to play. Streaks and round stats are stored in
    your browser. Corrections and questions go through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>
  <PlayCta slot="after" locale={defaultLocale} />
</LegalPage>
